---
title: Gluten-Free Garlic Bread
description: Two soft, crusty gluten-free batons baked from scratch, split and loaded with garlic parsley butter.
servings: 8
time required: 2 hours 10 minutes
prep time: 30 minutes
cook time: 45 minutes
difficulty: intermediate
course: side
diet: gluten-free, vegetarian, nut-free
cuisine: Italian
tags: bread, garlic-bread, gluten-free, side, baking, vegetarian, italian
locale: en_AU
---

Whisk together @white rice flour{160%g}, @potato starch{80%g}, @tapioca starch{60%g}, @xanthan gum{2%tsp} and @fine salt{1%tsp} in a #large bowl{} until evenly combined. This blend replaces the bread flour. Weigh the flours if you can — scooping packs the starch and gives a heavy, dry loaf.

Stir @caster sugar{2%tsp} and @dried yeast{7%g} into @warm water{250%ml}(lukewarm, about 40°C) in a #small bowl{} and stand for ~{10%minutes} until the surface is foamy.

Pour the yeast mixture into the dry ingredients with @olive oil{2%tbsp} and @white vinegar{2%tsp}. Beat with an #electric mixer{}(paddle attachment) or a sturdy #wooden spoon{} for ~{3%minutes} — the dough should look thick and sticky, like a soft batter rather than a kneadable dough. Cover and rest for ~{20%minutes} so the rice flour and starches can hydrate.

Line a #baking tray{} with #baking paper{}. With wet hands, divide the dough in half and shape each piece into a rough log about 24 cm long and 6 cm wide, leaving plenty of space between them — the logs spread as they prove.

Brush the logs with the remaining olive oil, cover loosely with oiled plastic wrap, and prove in a warm spot for ~{45%minutes}, until visibly puffed and a finger pressed gently into the surface leaves a slow-filling dent.

Heat the #oven{} to 220°C/425°F. Bake the batons for ~{30%minutes}, turning the tray halfway, until deep golden and the centre reads 96-98°C on a #probe thermometer{}. Gluten-free bread is not done when it merely looks brown: underbaked starch cools to a gummy crumb, so trust the temperature.

Slide the batons onto a #wire rack{} and cool for ~{20%minutes} before splitting — cutting hot bread tears the crumb.

Mash @unsalted butter{125%g}(softened) with @garlic{4%cloves}(crushed to a paste), @flat-leaf parsley{2%tbsp}(finely chopped), @?parmesan{40%g}(finely grated), @flaky salt{1/2%tsp} and a good grind of black pepper in a #small bowl{}. Taste and add more salt or garlic if you like it punchy.

Split the cooled batons lengthways with a #serrated knife{} and set them cut-side up on the tray. Spread the garlic butter generously over the cut faces, right to the edges.

Bake at 200°C/400°F for ~{10%minutes}, until the butter has melted in and the crust is crisp, then grill (broil) for ~{2%minutes} if you want the top bronzed — stand at the oven and watch it, because garlic butter scorches fast.

Cut into thick slices on the diagonal and serve hot, with any extra garlic butter spooned over.

> Serving suggestion: best alongside pasta, a roast chicken, or a bowl of soup; leftover slices reheat in a 180°C oven for ~{5%minutes}.

> Swaps to consider: @?roasted garlic{1%head}(squeezed from the cloves, for a sweeter, deeper spread); @?mozzarella{150%g}(shredded over the butter before baking for cheesy garlic bread); @?vegan butter{125%g} and @?nutritional yeast{2%tbsp} in place of the dairy; @?dried oregano{1%tsp} or @?chilli flakes{1/2%tsp} in the butter; @?psyllium husk{1%tbsp} added to the flour blend for a slightly more bread-like chew. Short on time? Split and butter a store-bought gluten-free baguette (Udi's or Schär both work) and skip straight to the baking step — but check the label, as not every "gluten-free" baguette is made in a wheat-free facility.

> Source blend: The garlic butter and split-and-bake method follow the classic Italian-American treatment described by Smitten Kitchen (https://smittenkitchen.com/2016/04/perfect-garlic-bread/) and Ambitious Kitchen (https://www.ambitiouskitchen.com/the-best-garlic-bread-recipe/), both of which use plenty of softened butter with fresh parsley, crusty split bread and a hot bake with an optional grill finish. The bake-then-split approach for gluten-free bread, and the store-bought baguette shortcut, are adapted from One Lovely Life (https://www.onelovelylife.com/gluten-free-garlic-bread/). The dough ratios and the 20-minute hydration rest are adapted from King Arthur Baking's Easy Gluten-Free Bread (https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/easy-gluten-free-bread-recipe), which uses a roughly 95% hydration dough and a 96-99°C internal temperature; this recipe lowers hydration to about 83% for a firmer baton that holds its shape on an open tray. The homemade rice/potato/tapioca blend with xanthan is the same family of blend used in this collection's Poppy Seed Bagels. The baton bake time is untested by this collection's owner and may need 5 minutes either way depending on the oven.
